{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You may want to reuse options that are intended to be passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Link"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"redirect"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"navigate"},{"type":"text","value":". In which case you may decide an object literal is a good way to represent options passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Link"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const dashboardLinkOptions = {\n  to: '/dashboard',\n  search: { search: '' },\n}\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  return <Link {...dashboardLinkOptions} />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are a few problems here. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dashboardLinkOptions.to"},{"type":"text","value":" is inferred as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"},{"type":"text","value":" which by default will resolve to every route when passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Link"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"navigate"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"redirect"},{"type":"text","value":" (this particular issue could be fixed by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"as const"},{"type":"text","value":"). The other issue here is we do not know "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dashboardLinkOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" even passes the type checker until it is spread into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Link"},{"type":"text","value":". We could very easily create incorrect navigation options and only when the options are spread into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Link"},{"type":"text","value":" do we know there is a type error."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-linkoptions-function-to-create-re-usable-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linkOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" function to create re-usable options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linkOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" is a function which type checks an object literal and returns the inferred input as is. This provides type safety on options exactly like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Link"},{"type":"text","value":" before it is used allowing for easier maintenance and re-usability. Our above example using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linkOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" looks like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const dashboardLinkOptions = linkOptions({\n  to: '/dashboard',\n  search: { search: '' },\n})\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  return <Link {...dashboardLinkOptions} />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This allows eager type checking of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dashboardLinkOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" which can then be re-used anywhere"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const dashboardLinkOptions = linkOptions({\n  to: '/dashboard',\n  search: { search: '' },\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/dashboard')({\n  component: DashboardComponent,\n  validateSearch: (input) => ({ search: input.search }),\n  beforeLoad: () => {\n    // can used in redirect\n    throw redirect(dashboardLinkOptions)\n  },\n})\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  const navigate = useNavigate()\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {/** can be used in navigate */}\n      <button onClick={() => navigate(dashboardLinkOptions)} />\n\n      {/** can be used in Link */}\n      <Link {...dashboardLinkOptions} />\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"an-array-of-linkoptions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An array of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linkOptions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When creating navigation you might loop over an array to construct a navigation bar. In which case "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linkOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" can be used to type check an array of object literals which are intended for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Link"},{"type":"text","value":" props"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const options = linkOptions([\n  {\n    to: '/dashboard',\n    label: 'Summary',\n    activeOptions: { exact: true },\n  },\n  {\n    to: '/dashboard/invoices',\n    label: 'Invoices',\n  },\n  {\n    to: '/dashboard/users',\n    label: 'Users',\n  },\n])\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <div className=\"flex items-center border-b\">\n        <h2 className=\"text-xl p-2\">Dashboard</h2>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"flex flex-wrap divide-x\">\n        {options.map((option) => {\n          return (\n            <Link\n              {...option}\n              key={option.to}\n              activeProps={{ className: `font-bold` }}\n              className=\"p-2\"\n            >\n              {option.label}\n            </Link>\n          )\n        })}\n      </div>\n      <hr />\n\n      <Outlet />\n    </>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The input of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linkOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" is inferred and returned, as shown with the use of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"label"},{"type":"text","value":" as this does not exist on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Link"},{"type":"text","value":" props"}]}],"headings":[{"id":"using-linkoptions-function-to-create-re-usable-options","text":"Using linkOptions function to create re-usable options","level":3},{"id":"an-array-of-linkoptions","text":"An array of linkOptions","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou may want to reuse options that are intended to be passed to `Link`, `redirect` or `navigate`. In which case you may decide an object literal is a good way to represent options passed to `Link`.\n\n```tsx\nconst dashboardLinkOptions = {\n  to: '/dashboard',\n  search: { search: '' },\n}\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  return <Link {...dashboardLinkOptions} />\n}\n```\n\nThere are a few problems here. `dashboardLinkOptions.to` is inferred as `string` which by default will resolve to every route when passed to `Link`, `navigate` or `redirect` (this particular issue could be fixed by `as const`). The other issue here is we do not know `dashboardLinkOptions` even passes the type checker until it is spread into `Link`. We could very easily create incorrect navigation options and only when the options are spread into `Link` do we know there is a type error.\n\n### Using `linkOptions` function to create re-usable options\n\n`linkOptions` is a function which type checks an object literal and returns the inferred input as is. This provides type safety on options exactly like `Link` before it is used allowing for easier maintenance and re-usability. Our above example using `linkOptions` looks like this:\n\n```tsx\nconst dashboardLinkOptions = linkOptions({\n  to: '/dashboard',\n  search: { search: '' },\n})\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  return <Link {...dashboardLinkOptions} />\n}\n```\n\nThis allows eager type checking of `dashboardLinkOptions` which can then be re-used anywhere\n\n```tsx\nconst dashboardLinkOptions = linkOptions({\n  to: '/dashboard',\n  search: { search: '' },\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/dashboard')({\n  component: DashboardComponent,\n  validateSearch: (input) => ({ search: input.search }),\n  beforeLoad: () => {\n    // can used in redirect\n    throw redirect(dashboardLinkOptions)\n  },\n})\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  const navigate = useNavigate()\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {/** can be used in navigate */}\n      <button onClick={() => navigate(dashboardLinkOptions)} />\n\n      {/** can be used in Link */}\n      <Link {...dashboardLinkOptions} />\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n### An array of `linkOptions`\n\nWhen creating navigation you might loop over an array to construct a navigation bar. In which case `linkOptions` can be used to type check an array of object literals which are intended for `Link` props\n\n```tsx\nconst options = linkOptions([\n  {\n    to: '/dashboard',\n    label: 'Summary',\n    activeOptions: { exact: true },\n  },\n  {\n    to: '/dashboard/invoices',\n    label: 'Invoices',\n  },\n  {\n    to: '/dashboard/users',\n    label: 'Users',\n  },\n])\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <div className=\"flex items-center border-b\">\n        <h2 className=\"text-xl p-2\">Dashboard</h2>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"flex flex-wrap divide-x\">\n        {options.map((option) => {\n          return (\n            <Link\n              {...option}\n              key={option.to}\n              activeProps={{ className: `font-bold` }}\n              className=\"p-2\"\n            >\n              {option.label}\n            </Link>\n          )\n        })}\n      </div>\n      <hr />\n\n      <Outlet />\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe input of `linkOptions` is inferred and returned, as shown with the use of `label` as this does not exist on `Link` props\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Link Options\n---\n\nYou may want to reuse options that are intended to be passed to `Link`, `redirect` or `navigate`. In which case you may decide an object literal is a good way to represent options passed to `Link`.\n\n```tsx\nconst dashboardLinkOptions = {\n  to: '/dashboard',\n  search: { search: '' },\n}\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  return <Link {...dashboardLinkOptions} />\n}\n```\n\nThere are a few problems here. `dashboardLinkOptions.to` is inferred as `string` which by default will resolve to every route when passed to `Link`, `navigate` or `redirect` (this particular issue could be fixed by `as const`). The other issue here is we do not know `dashboardLinkOptions` even passes the type checker until it is spread into `Link`. We could very easily create incorrect navigation options and only when the options are spread into `Link` do we know there is a type error.\n\n### Using `linkOptions` function to create re-usable options\n\n`linkOptions` is a function which type checks an object literal and returns the inferred input as is. This provides type safety on options exactly like `Link` before it is used allowing for easier maintenance and re-usability. Our above example using `linkOptions` looks like this:\n\n```tsx\nconst dashboardLinkOptions = linkOptions({\n  to: '/dashboard',\n  search: { search: '' },\n})\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  return <Link {...dashboardLinkOptions} />\n}\n```\n\nThis allows eager type checking of `dashboardLinkOptions` which can then be re-used anywhere\n\n```tsx\nconst dashboardLinkOptions = linkOptions({\n  to: '/dashboard',\n  search: { search: '' },\n})\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/dashboard')({\n  component: DashboardComponent,\n  validateSearch: (input) => ({ search: input.search }),\n  beforeLoad: () => {\n    // can used in redirect\n    throw redirect(dashboardLinkOptions)\n  },\n})\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  const navigate = useNavigate()\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {/** can be used in navigate */}\n      <button onClick={() => navigate(dashboardLinkOptions)} />\n\n      {/** can be used in Link */}\n      <Link {...dashboardLinkOptions} />\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n### An array of `linkOptions`\n\nWhen creating navigation you might loop over an array to construct a navigation bar. In which case `linkOptions` can be used to type check an array of object literals which are intended for `Link` props\n\n```tsx\nconst options = linkOptions([\n  {\n    to: '/dashboard',\n    label: 'Summary',\n    activeOptions: { exact: true },\n  },\n  {\n    to: '/dashboard/invoices',\n    label: 'Invoices',\n  },\n  {\n    to: '/dashboard/users',\n    label: 'Users',\n  },\n])\n\nfunction DashboardComponent() {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <div className=\"flex items-center border-b\">\n        <h2 className=\"text-xl p-2\">Dashboard</h2>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"flex flex-wrap divide-x\">\n        {options.map((option) => {\n          return (\n            <Link\n              {...option}\n              key={option.to}\n              activeProps={{ className: `font-bold` }}\n              className=\"p-2\"\n            >\n              {option.label}\n            </Link>\n          )\n        })}\n      </div>\n      <hr />\n\n      <Outlet />\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe input of `linkOptions` is inferred and returned, as shown with the use of `label` as this does not exist on `Link` props\n"}
